{
  "comment": "Conformance fixture for the job manifest (format job-manifest-v1): the signed record of what an agent delivered for one completion of a job. The TS SDK (sdk-typescript/src/job-manifest.ts) and the Rust SDK (sdk-rust/src/job_manifest.rs) MUST both reproduce the canonical bytes and the signatures here from the same seed, verify them, give the pinned reason for each invalid case, and compute the pinned reuse count for the prior/revision pair. sig covers signed_bytes_prefix + canonicalJSON(document without sig) and NOTHING ELSE; the prefix never appears in the document; sig is base64url. THE FIXTURE IS THE AUTHORITY: when a test against it fails, fix the implementation, and when the fixture itself is believed wrong, regenerate it with sdk-typescript/regen-job-manifest-fixture.mts alongside the change that made it wrong.",
  "version": 1,
  "spec": "job-manifest-v1 (the delivering agent's signed record of a completion's pieces); signing scheme as SPEC.md 5.3 / conformance/signature-tags.json",
  "identities": {
    "sender_seed": "SUAEGDPNTWXACIF45CA23BUY36RIMWZXW5LEHSEOK2ONS5JWJ7PBC2EWBA",
    "sender": "UBMBH4BK6EIHQFVMHLSABFSQXB3JXUFZOXVZQTIERO33AD4HHAI3D7QP",
    "recipient": "UAKQRYZBYFOC65OQZVJ3QPCIERDTRNNZPMOJUYXC3DGPRCDWSKD4HV5A",
    "comment": "The same published test vectors signature-tags.json, budget.json and inbound-protections.json use, deliberately: they are not real identities and control nothing, and reusing them keeps any new private-key-shaped string out of the repository. The sender is the delivering agent (the signer); the recipient is only ever the WRONG key here."
  },
